# public/build IS tracked. The production host has no Node.js, so Vite cannot
# run there; the compiled assets have to arrive with `git pull` or the site
# renders unstyled. Run `npm run build` and commit the result whenever you
# change anything under resources/css or resources/js.
/public/fonts-manifest.dev.json
/public/hot
/public/storage
# Host artifacts, not project files. deploy/setup-website.sh creates the
# public_html symlink on the server (Hostinger will not change a Web plan's
# document root), and deploy/queue-worker.sh is installed next to the application
# so cron can reach it. Without these, deploy.sh's dirty-tree guard blocks every
# deploy because of a file the deployment itself created.
#
# The worker is listed at every position it can legitimately land, because the
# guard keys on tracked-ness and a stray copy is not tracked. Getting this wrong
# does not fail loudly -- deploy.sh just refuses to run with "the working tree has
# uncommitted changes", naming a file the operator never touched.
#
# Which rule actually covers which position, measured with `git check-ignore -v`:
#
#   /queue-worker.sh             covers queue-worker.sh on its own. Anchored, since
#                                the deploy.sh default installs it at the repo root.
#   public/queue-worker.sh       covers itself on its own. Needed: public/ is
#                                tracked, so nothing else would match it. This is
#                                the Layout A position, where public_html is a
#                                symlink to public/.
#   public_html/queue-worker.sh  REDUNDANT today -- /public_html above already
#                                covers it. Kept deliberately: if /public_html is
#                                ever removed, this is the only thing stopping a
#                                worker copy from blocking every deploy. Do not
#                                cite it as the current protection; check with
#                                `git check-ignore -v` before removing anything.
#
# deploy.sh installs it and prints the matching cron command, so the file and the
# cron entry cannot drift apart unnoticed.
/public_html
/public_html.hostinger-backup
/queue-worker.sh
public/queue-worker.sh
public_html/queue-worker.sh
/storage/*.key
/storage/pail
/vendor
_ide_helper.php
Homestead.json
Homestead.yaml
Thumbs.db
/.claude
/DESIGN.md
